<article class="ax-lab ax-methods">
<span class="ax-kicker">BIOHYBRID AXON RESOLUTION / STAGE 2</span><h1>MODALITIES & SELECTIVITY: METHODS</h1>
<p><a href="../">Open coupled laboratory</a> · <a href="../questions/">Scientific questions</a> · <a href="../validation.json">Full numerical report</a></p>
<div class="ax-boundary"><strong>{report.completeStage2?'Numerical Stage 2 release gates passed.':'Development checkpoint: Stage 2 release gates remain open.'}</strong><p>Active myelinated cables, physical wells, magnetic induction and photon-driven membrane currents are calculated. Engineered ganglion physiology, survival, growth, chronic response and manufacturing are uncalibrated. Each introduced bipolar cell has its own imposed projections; this B3 experiment contains no organoid-to-host junction.</p></div>
<h2>01 · Scope and identity</h2>
<p>Every declared cell, channel and sampled amplitude is solved. The worker runs matched zero-input, regional active-conductance removal, soma-only removal and whole-cell removal controls. Optical runs add an opsin block that keeps the light field and all native conductances. Whole-cell native silencing keeps the photocurrent. All interventions begin from the same saved native state. A change in controls cannot relabel an earlier result.</p>
<p>Original host identities H and introduced identities E remain distinct. Complete ganglion resection makes original-host continuity and Y<sub>axon</sub> zero. An electrically recruited E output is not restored H continuity. The matched no-device condition has the same resection and no introduced cells, well walls or drive. Growth and target reinnervation are not inferred.</p>
<h2>02 · Three physical drives</h2>
<p><b>Electrode · mathematical.</b> The Stage 1 conforming tetrahedral FEM resolves finite source/return contacts, insulating wells, conducting apertures, anisotropic endoneurium and perineurium. Charge-balanced biphasic drive uses the same physical transfer and double-cable model. Geometric phase charge is reported; electrode polarization and electrochemical safety are unsolved.</p>
<pre>{'∇ · (σ ∇φ) = 0 with explicit current boundary loads\nφe(s,t) = T(s) I(t)\nCk = cable potential range at target / maximum off-target range'}</pre>
<p><b>Microcoil · mathematical formulation, hypothetical geometry.</b> Analytic finite segments include every winding and return segment. The complete finite-width winding lies inside the pre-existing excluded wall; a wire is never silently placed in conducting tissue. The primary electric field drives an independently assembled conservative FEM load. Charge redistribution at the tissue and insulation boundaries adds a secondary scalar field.</p>
<pre>{'A(r) = μ0 I/(4π) ∫wire dl′ / |r − r′|\nEp = −∂A/∂t\nKij = ∫ grad(Ni) · σ grad(Nj) dV\nbi = ∫ grad(Ni) · σ Ep dV\nK φ = b\nψ(s) = φ(s) + (dI/dt) ∫path A1 · dl\nEparallel = −dψ/ds; activation depends on its spatial variation.'}</pre>
<p>The Stage 2 induction solver applies symmetric diagonal equilibration to the same physical stiffness matrix. Each axial-column block is factored exactly for the preconditioner. PCG uses a two-component solution and error-free products when observing the original physical residual. Iterative refinement uses that original residual, rather than only the rounded scaled matrix, and restarts the search direction. No physical coefficient is shifted. The true-residual limit remains 10⁻¹⁰, the recurrence target 2 × 10⁻¹² and the iteration cap 16,000. The fine-mesh reciprocal recording solve uses the same precision algebra with the original finite source/return contact load. Its agreement with the inherited contact solver is checked separately, and production recording is exercised by the spatial study. Stage 1 source is unchanged.</p>
<p>For a closed winding, ∇·A = 0. In each constant tensor σ = diag(t,t,z), the source is assembled as interface flux plus the remaining longitudinal anisotropic volume integral. Internal equal-conductivity faces cancel before quadrature. This avoids numerically integrating a large divergence-free transverse field against an imperfect volume rule.</p>
<pre>{'bi per unit slew = −t ∮ Ni A1 · n dS − (z−t) ∫ (∂z Ni) A1,z dV\nFace integration: 8 × 8 Gauss–Duffy rule\nAnisotropic volume integration: Keast 11-point degree-four rule\nMagnetic parent mesh: radial factor 2\nProduction: planar subdivision 1, axial factor 2\nPlanar reference: subdivision 2 at axial 2\nAxial reference: factor 3 at planar subdivision 1'}</pre>
<p>A separate Stage 2 mesh generator subdivides fixed parent triangles. Shared edge midpoints inherit the same warped physical coordinates, material regions and polygonal interfaces. Refinement does not regenerate the background triangulation or move a finite contact. Stage 1 source is untouched. Both the original seed-2048 cohort and current seed-4079 cohort must pass every per-cell directional comparison. The largest planar reference is offline only: its resource ceiling is 3.6 million raw vertices, 22 million tetrahedra, 75 million matrix slots and a 2.7 GB working-memory estimate. The browser retains its original 1.8 million, 11 million, 60 million and 2.1 GB ceilings. Physical assembly and numerical acceptance are identical. These are admission estimates, not guaranteed peak memory. The independent NumPy/SciPy reference rebuilds physical tetrahedron gradients, conductivity stiffness and face fluxes, using a sorted-face algorithm rather than the production face map. It does not import the production matrix or source vector. Algebraic normalization is restored to the original physical magnitude before residual and potential comparisons. No comparison stimulus, trace amplitude or timing is fitted.</p>
<p>The local well boundary retains the inherited 12 angular sectors plus the exact contact-edge angles. Planar subdivision resolves the field on that fixed polygonal boundary; it does not turn the boundary into a true circle or certify curved-surface geometry error. The directional transfer limits therefore apply to the declared polygonal model and the tested cohorts. Axial refinement also resolves the sampled soma and stem profile. Neither comparison establishes a geometry-independent error bound.</p>
<p>A nonmagnetic wall does not shield the primary magnetic field. It still affects secondary electric fields through the conductivity boundary. The path potential is valid for this straight chain. Transverse soma polarization, branched-cycle fields, skin effects and driver parasitics are unresolved. The coupled axis-aligned sidewall coil can couple very weakly; a negative result for it is not a negative result for all magnetic geometries. Wire resistance, approximate inductance, pulse energy and aggregate drive power remain visible.</p>
<p><b>Optical · mathematical transport, hypothetical tissue.</b> Seeded Lambertian packets scatter with the Henyey–Greenstein law, absorb in a uniform medium and reflect from an opaque specular liner. Geometric apertures determine escape. Weighted path length in each thin spherical soma band gives local scalar fluence. The physical cell and field anatomy are inherited unchanged.</p>
<pre>{'Φband / Pemitted = Σhistories(weight × path length in band) / (N × shell volume)\nIopsin = gmax × area × [10.6408 − 14.6408 exp(−Vm / 42.7671)] × (O1 + 0.1 O2)\nC1 ⇄ O1 ⇄ O2 ⇄ C2 → C1\nProbability-conserving Markov update; voltage-dependent rate and current equations.\nThe photocurrent enters only the axolemma balance, never the outer/periaxonal balance.'}</pre>
<p>The H134R rates follow Williams et al. and the ModelDB implementation. Those data do not fit engineered human ganglion cells. Expression density is a hypothetical control. Transport supplies local exposure, so the additional loss divisor is one. Native conductances and opsin states interact during every time step; responses are not assigned from a light threshold.</p>
<h2>03 · Optical uncertainty and spectral multiplexing</h2>
<p>Each photon is an independent bounded contribution. The maximum shell chord and maximum packet-retention ratio bound its total contribution. A two-sided empirical Bernstein interval allocates a family error probability across all requested channels and bands. Zero detected off-target photons produce a positive upper bound. Discarded packet weight remains in the energy budget and worst-case confidence tail.</p>
<p>Lower, nominal and upper exposure vectors are passed through the nonlinear cable. If recruitment differs, the headline is withheld. Agreement of three probes does not enclose every interior nonlinear response and is never described as a rigorous firing guarantee. Shell thickness, finite-domain loss, optical coefficients and biological uncertainty are separate from packet-sampling uncertainty.</p>
<p>The coupled spectral experiment assigns a named opsin identity to every introduced cell and runs every physical source at each declared wavelength. The two-family baseline uses ChR2 and Chrimson; the third family adds vf-Chrimson with almost the same action spectrum as Chrimson. The fourth is an explicitly hypothetical green construct. Near-identical spectra can yield shared activation and no additional independent channels. No spectral multiplier is assumed.</p>
<p>ChR2, Chrimson and vf-Chrimson use distinct Bansal four-state parameter sets transcribed from the pinned Cleo implementation. They do not reuse the Williams H134R mechanism. Published maximum conductance is a whole-cell quantity in nS; it is multiplied by the exposed hypothetical expression factor and partitioned by soma-band area. Refining the membrane mesh therefore does not add expression.</p>
<pre>{`φeffective = Σλ Iλ [mW/mm²] × 10⁻³ × λ [m] / (h c) × wλ
Hp = φeffective^p / (φeffective^p + φm^p)
Ga1 = k1 Hp; Ga2 = k2 Hp
Gf = Gf0 + kf Hq; Gb = Gb0 + kb Hq
Ioutward [nA] = g0 [nS] × expression × area fraction × (O1 + γ O2) × (Vm − E) / 1000`}</pre>
<p>Photons from all supplied bins are summed before the nonlinear Hill rates. Raw equal-photon Chrimson action measurements are used once as photon-response weights. The original ChR2 action-curve normalization remains uncertain: the declared equal-power assumption converts its sensitivity by λreference/λ, and an alternate equal-photon interpretation is rerun for every pair. No action spectrum is extrapolated. The shared supported range is 470–560 nm, so a red-sensitive opsin does not justify inventing a zero ChR2 tail at 590 nm.</p>
<p>Action-weighted photon flux is an estimated coupling convention, not an absorption cross section. Room-temperature rates are applied to the 37 °C cable without a validated Q10. Every pair receives separate 0.5× and 2× expression and kinetic-rate probes. These retain the physical light field and native conductances. Nominal results remain conditional on these assumptions; a changed sensitivity classification is shown explicitly.</p>
<p>Transport is rerun independently for each wavelength using μa(λ) = μa(470) (λ/470)<sup>−aa</sup> and μs(λ) = μs(470) (λ/470)<sup>−as</sup>. Coefficients and exponents are hypothetical, exposed controls. The default absorption exponent is zero and scattering exponent is one. The liner remains a wavelength-independent specular approximation. Separate monochromatic source trials do not establish simultaneous multi-source controllability.</p>
